  Get gauntlets of power, add dust Elbereths and even at XL:7 with +0
daggers you can deal out 2.5 x 14 = 35 damage per fling.

  Can wear a shield, up to 9 more AC.  In the late game when mobbed that
extra 9AC will cut an average of 4.5 hp of damage from each successful
attack.  That can easily save 20 Hp per turn.  When completely
surrounded by tough monsters getting multiple attacks per turn (say, on
the planes of Fire or Air) could easily save 40 Hp per turn.  Up to
about 80 hp max per turn.  


  Magicbane is great for Rogues because when non-cursed it can engrave
Elbereth semipermanently with 100% success in a single turn. (Subject to
blindness, confusion, hallucination, being on solid ground, not
levitating, etc.)  Elbereth fearing monsters will be scared and thus
subject to the back stab bonus.  

  Since the back stab bonus is per-hit it can be multiplied an average
of 2.5 by flinging Expert daggers.  Rogues can get to Expert, which
averages 2.0 daggers per fling.  Rogues get a role bonus dagger chance
per fling for a total average of 2.5 per fling.  Range is Strength/2.

  Think of it as long-range 2.5 weaponing.  And you can wear a shield,
which can give up to 9 more AC with a +7 Elven shield.  (Elven armor can
be safely enchanted when its at +5.)

  Note that it doesn't have to be Magicbane.  Any non-cursed athame can
do the 100% certain semi-permanent one-turn engraving.  A plain athame
can also be wished for, guaranteed.  The athame doesn't have to be
wielded, although Magicbane probably should be.  Wield the best dagger
you find (probably +2 or so, although I've found +4 before).

  Elbereth only works on adjacent squares (although it does all adjacent
squares).  Other methods of scaring monsters include mirrors, spell of
cause fear, leather drums, tooled horns, (magic horns?), drum of
earthquake, scroll of Scare Monster (keep holy water handy to re-bless).

  Elbereth can also be engraved with wands of digging, fire, and
lightning.  Even in the dust using fingers ('-').

  Note that monsters also turn to flee when they've taken enough damage.
It's a nice feeling when that happens in the middle of a 4 dagger fling,
so that the last two daggers get the back stab bonus.
  

